      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the area of the parallelogram generated by two vectors, we use the fact that the area is the magnitude of the cross product of the two vectors.\n\nGiven vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), where\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwe can use the formula for the cross product:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n3 & 1 & -2 \\\\\n1 & -3 & 4\n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe expand this determinant using the first row:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i} \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} - \\mathbf{j} \\begin{vmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} + \\mathbf{k} \\begin{vmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nCalculating each minor:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} = (1)(4) - (-2)(-3) = 4 - 6 = -2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} = (3)(4) - (-2)(1) = 12 + 2 = 14,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\end{vmatrix} = (3)(-3) - (1)(1) = -9 - 1 = -10.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values back into the expansion:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i}(-2) - \\mathbf{j}(14) + \\mathbf{k}(-10) = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of this cross product vector is:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \\sqrt{300} = \\sqrt{100 \\times 3} = 10\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram is \\(10\\sqrt{3}\\). The final answer is \\(\\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
